---
/**
 * WHO WE DEPLOY HUB — the six families, and the eight pages behind them.
 *
 * The cards are the families, because that is what the nav lists. Each one
 * names the job titles it covers — read from ROLE_FAMILIES, so the card and
 * the dropdown cannot disagree — and the page or pages it sits over.
 */
import Prototype from "../layouts/Prototype.astro";
import Block from "../components/Block.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/base";
import { B } from "../data/nav";
import { ROLE_FAMILIES, FAMILY_STATS } from "../data/nav-options";
import type { Block as BlockType } from "../lib/types";

/** What each family's page is for, keyed by the route the nav points at. */
const ANGLE: Record<string, string> = {
  "/roles/cnc-operators": "The hardest seats on the floor to fill. Process runs as a card row.",
  "/roles/welders": "Weld processes and a who-brings-what split. Process runs as a chip strip.",
  "/roles/assemblers-production": "Scale, not depth — a few seats to a whole line. Process runs as a table.",
  "/roles/forklift-operators": "Equipment classes and dock throughput. Process runs as a vertical rail.",
  "/roles/packaging-operations": "Ship windows and retail stakes. Process runs as a dot stepper.",
  "/roles/sanitation-crews": "The shift that makes every other shift possible. Process runs as a definition list.",
};

/** What is on each page a family covers without the nav pointing at it. */
const COVERED: Record<string, string> = {
  Machinists: "Manual, toolroom and maintenance work. Written, wireframed, and now unreachable from the menu.",
  "Warehouse &amp; Logistics": "On the build sheet. No copy deck, no page, and no route decided — it still resolves to this hub.",
};

const cards = ROLE_FAMILIES.map((f) => {
  const route = f.pages[0].route;
  const extra = f.pages.slice(1);
  const behind = [
    `<b>${f.pages[0].label}</b>`,
    ...extra.map((pg) => `${pg.label}${pg.written ? "" : " · no copy yet"}`),
  ].join(" + ");
  return `<a class="card" style="text-decoration:none;display:block" href="${u(route)}">
     <h3 style="margin-bottom:10px">${f.label}</h3>
     <p style="font-size:13.5px;color:var(--spec);margin-bottom:12px">${f.roles.join(" · ")}</p>
     <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:12px">${ANGLE[route] ?? ""}</p>
     <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--spec)">Behind it: ${behind}</p>
     <span class="tag build">${extra.length ? "FAMILY · 2 PAGES" : "BUILD · WIREFRAMED"}</span></a>`;
}).join("");

/* Read off the families themselves — the second page of any family that has
   one — so a family gaining or losing a page cannot leave this table behind. */
const coveredRows = ROLE_FAMILIES.flatMap((f) =>
  f.pages.slice(1).map(
    (pg) =>
      `<tr><th>${pg.label}${pg.written ? "" : " <span style=\"color:var(--spec)\">· no copy yet</span>"}</th><td>${
        COVERED[pg.label] ?? ""
      }</td><td class="hl">${f.label}</td></tr>`,
  ),
).join("");

const blocks: BlockType[] = [
  {
    n: "WHO WE DEPLOY · THE SIX FAMILIES",
    h: `
  <h1>Who We Deploy</h1>
  <p class="lead">${FAMILY_STATS.families} families over ${FAMILY_STATS.pages} pages. The families are what the nav lists and what each page is now titled; the job titles under each are illustration, not links.</p>
  <p class="lead"><b>Two families cover two pages each.</b> Machining &amp; Precision Manufacturing sits over CNC Operators and Machinists; Material Handling &amp; Warehouse Operations over Forklift Operators and the unwritten Warehouse &amp; Logistics. The nav points at the first of each pair, so the second has no route into it from the menu.</p>
  <div class="g3" style="margin-top:30px">${cards}</div>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The two pages with no nav slot</h3>
  <div class="tscroll"><table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>What is on it</th><th class="hl">Covered by</th></tr></thead><tbody>${coveredRows}</tbody></table></div>
  <div class="note stop"><b>These are the two merges the family list implies, and neither has been made.</b> Fold each into its family page, or give the family two pages and decide which the nav points at. <b>Until one of those happens, written copy sits on a page nothing links to.</b></div>`,
    spec: `<b>Elementor:</b> Container (3-col) · EK Image Box ×6. This hub is a spoke off <a href="${u("/how-it-works")}">How It Works</a>; every role page links back to it, to the pillar, and to its siblings.`,
  },

  {
    cls: "wash",
    n: "OPEN ITEM · THE SLUG PATTERN",
    h: `
  <div class="note stop" style="margin-top:0"><b>Every one of the seven decks carries the same open item: confirm the <code>/roles/</code> slug pattern against the site IA deck before the cluster builds.</b> The industries cluster uses <code>/industries/</code>, so the convention is consistent as drafted — but it has never actually been signed off, and eight URLs is an expensive thing to change after publication.</div>`,
    spec: `Decide this at structure sign-off, with the industries slugs, in one pass.`,
  },

  {
    n: "SETTLED · TWO NAMES DRIFTED",
    h: `
  <div class="tscroll"><table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Agreed sub-nav label</th><th>Copy deck title</th><th class="hl">Which to use</th></tr></thead><tbody>
   <tr><th>Welders (travel crews)</th><td>Welders &amp; Fabricators</td><td class="hl">Deck — fabricators are a named role on the page</td></tr>
   <tr><th>Packaging Operators</th><td>Packaging Operations</td><td class="hl">Deck — the page covers the line, not just the operator</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <div class="note"><b>Neither name is in play any more.</b> The nav lists the six families, and the page H1s now follow the family too — so Welders and Packaging are job titles inside a page rather than the name of one. <b>The drift is closed at both ends.</b> What it leaves behind is a bigger copy question, flagged on every page: the H1, title and meta no longer match the approved decks.</div>`,
    spec: `The family labels are settled and the dropdown is built from them. <b>The decks are what now need re-issuing</b>, or the family titles need to go back to the deck wording — one or the other.`,
  },

  {
    cls: "wash",
    n: "OPEN ITEM · KEYWORD SPLITS TO HOLD",
    h: `
  <p class="lead">Three pairs of pages sit close enough to compete with each other in search. The decks resolve each one, and the resolutions only hold if the cross-links get built.</p>
  <div class="g3" style="margin-top:26px">
   <div class="card"><h3>CNC Operators ↔ Machinists</h3><p style="margin:0">The split still holds — CNC terms on one page, manual, toolroom and maintenance on the other — but <b>one family now sits over both and the nav points only at the first</b>. The cross-links matter more than they did, because they are the only route to the second.</p></div>
   <div class="card"><h3>Assemblers ↔ Packaging</h3><p style="margin:0">Packaging keywords moved off the Assemblers page to Packaging Operations, which keeps assembler, production and general labor. The Assemblers deck was revised in step.</p></div>
   <div class="card"><h3>Sanitation ↔ Food &amp; Beverage</h3><p style="margin:0">Sanitation owns sanitation, deep-clean and wash-down staffing; the industry page keeps the plant-level framing.</p></div>
  </div>`,
    spec: `These are internal-linking rules, not copy. Worth writing into the build sheet so they survive the hand-off.`,
  },

  {
    n: "SHARED ASSET GAP",
    h: `
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:0"><b>The role cluster needs one icon set and seven form illustrations before it can go live.</b> Every page specs 96×96 line icons in the existing CSTL style — five or six per page, roughly forty in total — plus a 1200×1200 isometric form illustration each, all specified as distinct subjects. On top of that: seven hero photographs, five bespoke infographics and four case images. <b>Commission the icon set once, as a set.</b></div>`,
    spec: `Only the Welders resource card reuses an existing asset (the eBook cover from the live pillar). Everything else on these seven pages is new.`,
  },

  {
    cls: "wash",
    n: "SETTLED · THE SUB-NAV SLOT",
    h: `
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:0"><b>Settled: the entry is <i>Who We Deploy</i>, and it lists the six families.</b> The objection was a sub-nav entry that read as a list of job titles. Families answer it — the titles are still on the page, under a heading that is a capability rather than a vacancy.</div>
  <div class="note"><b>The comparison that got there is still up.</b> Renaming the entry, grouping the list under headings, two ways of adding a second level, a two-pane panel, and two mega menus that move the question to the primary nav — every one of them opens. <a href="${u("/nav-options")}">The comparison →</a> &middot; <a href="${u("/nav-h-sample")}">The menu as a site header →</a> &middot; <a href="${u("/nav-layers")}">Colour treatments for its tiers →</a></div>
  <div class="note stop"><b>What is still open is across-or-down, not the label.</b> If the mega menu goes ahead, the CSTL sub-nav either shrinks to a few in-section links or goes away, and this entry goes with it.</div>`,
    spec: `The label and the groupings are decided. <b>Whether the section is reached from a sub-nav at all is not.</b>`,
  },
];
---

<Prototype
  title="Who We Deploy"
  crumb="Solutions › CSTL › Who We Deploy"
  url={`${B}roles/`}
  status="build"
  active="/roles"
>
  {blocks.map((b) => <Block {...b} />)}
</Prototype>
